      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Chronic pain and opioid consumption may trigger diffuse hyperalgesia, but their relative contributions to pain vulnerability remain unclear."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "To assess preoperative opioid-induced hyperalgesia and its postoperative clinical consequences in patients with chronic pain scheduled for orthopaedic surgery."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "A prospective observational study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTINGS",
          "content": "Raymond Poincare teaching hospital."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS",
          "content": "Adults with or without long-term opioid treatment, scheduled for orthopaedic surgery."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PRIMARY OUTCOME MEASURE",
          "content": "Preoperative hyperalgesia was assessed with eight quantitative sensory tests, in a pain-free zone."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SECONDARY OUTCOME MEASURES",
          "content": "Postoperative morphine consumption and pain intensity were evaluated using a numerical rating scale (NRS) in the recovery room and during the first 72\u200ah."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "We analysed results from 68 patients (28 opioid-treated patients and 40 controls). Mean daily opioid consumption was 42\u200a±\u200a25\u200amg of morphine equivalent. The opioid-treated group displayed significantly higher levels of preoperative hyperalgesia in three tests: heat tolerance threshold (47.1°C vs. 48.4°C; P\u200a=\u200a0.045), duration of tolerance to a 47°C stimulus (40.2 vs. 51.1\u200as; P\u200a=\u200a0.03) and mechanical temporal summation [1.79 vs. 1.02 (ΔNRS10-1); P\u200a=\u200a0.036]. Patients in the opioid-treated group consumed more morphine (19.1 vs. 9.38\u200amg; P\u200a=\u200a0.001), had a higher pain intensity (7.6 vs. 5.5; P\u200a=\u200a0.001) in the recovery room and a higher cumulative morphine dose at 72\u200ah (39.8 vs. 25.6\u200amg; P\u200a=\u200a0.02)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Chronic pain patients treated with low doses of opioid had hyperalgesia before surgery. These results highlight the need to personalise the management of patients treated with opioids before surgery."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "TRIAL REGISTRATION",
          "content": "ID-RCB 2011-A00304-37."
        }
      ]},
